  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{\u2032}={f}^{\u2032}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation",
  "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x)}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x \\\\  & \\ne 2\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x$ and ${y}^{\u2032}=\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u20612x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u20612x & \\ne \\operatorname{sin}\u20612x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
